make sure that you  don't overfit and basically thereby  apply a feature selection  so this way we score all the pairs  within the blocks and  go to the next step  so how do we how do we train this uh  classification model  you could uh take a bunch of  of data samples or a data sample and  then  label all the combinations in there or  so but that's a lot of work and most  often  the majority of the pairs are not a  match  so that's the reason why we use active  learning here  as implemented by the modal package  um in active learning you create an  interactive session where the user  enters for each pair that is presented  whether it is a match or not  and it's this is a very efficient way to  collect training data  and  quickly have a trained model  so how it works you present all the  pairs that you found in your blocking  stage  and then  the model is applied to the data and  then the  data sample so the pair in this case  that the model is most uncertain about  is presented to the user the user then  gives a yes or no whether it's a match  or not that data point is added to the  training data the model is retrained and  the process continues and you continue  doing that until you have a converged  model  and this  generally with the dupli by you have a  converged model in like about 20 30 of  these steps it goes really fast  the question then is how does how does  the dupli by or modal i should say how  does it measure the uncertainty and  that's actually pretty simple it's just  one minus the probability of the most  probable class  so let me show that in this  in this little plot there are three data  points the amber dots indicate the data  points and the prediction of the model  and as you can see the most left and the  most right data points there is a small  uncertainty so there is the the score is  close to close to one  but the point in the middle has a much  larger uncertainty so when you do active  learning this will be the data point  that will be presented to the user and  the user should then give input whether  it's a  it's a zero or a one whether it's a  match or not  let's have a look how this active  learning works on a um yeah  a data set and for this we use the north  carolina voter registry data set it's a  data set that is more often used for  deduplication and is provided by  professor erhard  the data set consists of three columns  the name the suburb and the postcode  and there are a lot of duplicates in it  as you can see the first two are most  likely duplicates there are two  representations of the same person  so chemical thomas is written in in two  different ways here  then he lives in charlotte suburb and  there is also a typo and there's a g  instead of a nine or an ocr error i  don't know what the sources of this data  and the task is here to  identify which rows belong to each other  which rows belong to the same  real world entity and person in this  case  so let's have a look how this  uncertainty  evolves over the training of the d  duplicate instance  here we look at an uncertainty  map on the horizontal axis you see the  similarity of the names and on the  vertical axis you see the similarity of  the suburbs because i wanted to create a  two-dimensional plot i could only use  two columns and just one  similarity metric per per column  then on the bottom left you see the  dissimilar names and the dissimilar  suburbs so these are the cases where  most likely it's not a match  and on the top right you see the more  similar names and the more similar  suburbs so there's a larger probability  that  the bear is image  then the colors indicate the uncertainty  so on the bottom left you see a lot of  green they're very low uncertainty and  that is logical because if there is no  similarity in the name and no similarity  in the suburb it's most likely not image  on the top right you see that there is  much there are much less dots but  there's a lot of white and that is just  because there are not so many matches in  the data  but there you also see some some  greenish colors here  and that's also logical because if there  is very large similarity in both the  names and the suburbs it's most likely a  match  and in the region in between you see a  lot of red so there is the high  uncertainty so these are the pairs that  will be presented to the user  this is the uncertainty heat map after  two label pairs  then the  duplify will present the most  uncertain pair to the user and the user  needs to enter whether it's a match or  not and the parent is presented is shown  here in the in the plot  so in this case i would say this this is  a match although there are some typos in  it after four pairs  the plot looks like this and if you  continue labeling you see over time  that the the area of the red area so the  uncertain area gets much smaller and  after 50 labeled pairs the mole is  converged  so now you see that really on the bottom  left of course it's a lot of green so  there is no uncertainty these are just  no patches and on the top right you see  there is also a good green area  indicating that the model is not unsure  so it's sure about the  positive  the matches of the pairs  okay so we first started with the  blocking stage then within the blocks we  calculate the similarities of the pairs  they are using this interactive learning  and the next thing that we do is that we  are going to create a network of pairs  and probabilities  for this we use network x  and  in this graph that we create every node  is a representation it's basically a row  in our data set and every edge between  the nodes is the  is the pair that is that is scored and  the edge weights the numbers you see  in the lines is the probability that was  output by the logistic regression model  the first thing after creating this  network that we do is  we apply the connected components  algorithm and the connected components  algorithm  um  distinguishes the subgraphs in the graph 